.\" tclrsall(1) — comprehensive all-in-one man page for tclrs.
.\" Modeled after zshall(1): every page in one document.
.\" Render with:  man -l man/man1/tclrsall.1
.\" Install:      cp man/man1/tclrsall.1 /usr/local/share/man/man1/
.TH TCLRSALL 1 "2026-07-28" "tclrs 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tclrsall \- the tclrs comprehensive reference (invocation, the twelve rules,
commands, expressions, the REPL, the editor servers, inline Rust, compilation)
.SH OVERVIEW
This is the all-in-one
.B tclrs
reference. For the concise command page see
.BR tclrs (1).
.PP
.B tclrs
runs Tcl by parsing it once and lowering each command to
.B fusevm
bytecode \(em the same bytecode the other fusevm frontends emit, among them
.BR zshrs ", " stryke ", " awkrs ", " vimlrs " and " elisprs .
There is no interpreter loop and no code generator in this crate: execution,
the three-tier Cranelift JIT and the ahead-of-time compiler all belong to the
VM.
.PP
The reference implementation is
.BR tclsh (1)
9.0.4. Behavior is ported from it rather than reinvented, and the test suite
runs every program through both and compares the output byte for byte \(em no
expected output in the repository is written by hand.
.SH INVOCATION
.TP
.B tclrs \fIFILE\fR [\fIarg\fR ...]
Run a script file. One script: the first failure ends it and exits 1.
.TP
.B tclrs \-c \fISCRIPT\fR [\fIarg\fR ...]
Run the script given on the command line.
.TP
.B tclrs
Read from standard input. A pipe is a sequence of commands, each evaluated as
it completes, and end of input exits 0. A terminal is the REPL below.
.PP
.BR \-\-aot ", " \-\-aot\-object ", " \-\-tiers ", " \-\-dump\-tokens ", "
.BR \-\-dump\-ast ", " \-\-disasm ", " \-\-lsp ", " \-\-dap ", " \-\-help ", "
.B \-\-version
are described in
.BR tclrs (1).
.SH "THE TWELVE RULES"
Tcl's grammar is twelve substitution rules, and the parser implements all
twelve: command separation by newline and
.RB \(dq ; \(dq,
words separated by whitespace, double quotes, braces, command substitution
.RB \(dq [] \(dq,
variable substitution
.RB \(dq $ \(dq
including array elements, backslash substitution, and comments at a command's
first word. Rule 5,
.RB \(dq {*} \(dq
argument expansion, is read and recorded on the word, but a command carrying one
is refused when it is lowered rather than approximated.
.PP
A braced word is literal by construction, and tclrs uses that: an
.BR if ", " while ", " for " or " foreach
body, and a braced
.B expr
expression, is fully known while compiling, so it is lowered to bytecode once
instead of being re-parsed on every evaluation.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B Variables
.BR set ", " unset ", " incr ", " append ", " global ", " variable .
A procedure's variables are frame slots; a script's are VM globals.
.TP
.B Control flow
.BR if ", " while ", " for ", " foreach ", " switch ", " break ", "
.BR continue ", " return ", " catch ", " error .
Every loop is emitted rotated \(em entered at the test, closed by a conditional
backward branch \(em because that is the shape the tracing JIT installs a trace
for.
.TP
.B Procedures
.BR proc .
Parameters and locals are frame slots; defaults and a trailing
.B args
are resolved at the call site.
.TP
.B Coroutines
.BR coroutine ", " yield ", " yieldto .
A coroutine is a second VM over the same chunk, with the transfer owned by the
driver.
.TP
.B "The event loop"
.BR after ", " update ", " vwait .
The registry of pending scripts belongs to the interpreter, as Tcl's does. A
default build's only event sources are those scripts; a
.B \-\-features\ tk
build also pumps the ported notifier, where Tk's window and file events arrive.
.TP
.B Scope
.BR uplevel ", " upvar ", " apply .
.B uplevel
resolves its level when the command runs and serves a target that is the global
level;
.B upvar
binds
.B #0
while the script is read; a written-out lambda is compiled as an anonymous
procedure with its own frame slots. A level that is another procedure's
activation is refused rather than served against the wrong variables.
.TP
.B Introspection
.BR info .
Most subcommands are answered while compiling, because most of what
.B info
asks has a compile-time answer here.
.TP
.B Lists
.BR list ", " llength ", " lindex ", " lappend ", " lrange ", " lreverse ", "
.BR linsert ", " lreplace ", " lsearch ", " lsort ", " join ", " split ", "
.BR concat .
.B lsort
reproduces the reference merge sort element for element, because with
.B \-unique
the algorithm decides which of two equal elements survives.
.TP
.B Strings
.BR format ", " append ,
and the
.B string
ensemble.
.TP
.B Associative data
The
.B array
ensemble (over a variable) and the
.B dict
ensemble (over a value).
.PP
.BR eval ", " puts " and " expr
complete the set. The generated reference at
https://menketechnologies.github.io/tclrs/reference.html lists every command,
every ensemble subcommand with whether it is implemented, and the operator
ladder \(em all emitted from the compiler's own tables.
.SH EXPRESSIONS
.B expr
compiles its argument rather than interpreting it. The comparisons, the
bitwise and shift operators, the short-circuiting
.RB \(dq && \(dq
and
.RB \(dq || \(dq,
and
.BR + " " \- " " *
lower to native fusevm ops. Only the operators whose Tcl meaning differs from
the VM's generic one \(em
.RB \(dq / \(dq,
.RB \(dq % \(dq
and
.RB \(dq ** \(dq,
which floor toward negative infinity and stay integral for integral operands
\(em take a frontend extension op.
.PP
Integers are 64-bit here where Tcl's are arbitrary-precision: an overflow is an
error, raised by the numeric hook, rather than a wrapped result. The chunk asks
fusevm for the overflow-checked lowering so the JIT and the AOT compiler agree
with the interpreter about it.
.SH "THE REPL"
A terminal gets a reedline editor. Multi-line editing is decided by the parser
itself \(em a command with a brace, quote or bracket still open keeps the
editor on the same buffer \(em so the editor and the evaluator cannot disagree
about where a command ends. Tab completes command names, an ensemble's
subcommands, the session's procedures, and variables after
.RB \(dq $ \(dq.
History is
.IR ~/.tclrs/history .
.PP
A procedure is compiled into the chunk of the script that defines it, so it
would otherwise last exactly one line; the REPL keeps the text of each
definition and prefixes the set to every later evaluation, which is what makes
a procedure answer on the line after it was written. Coroutines are not carried
that way, since replaying one would run its body again.
.PP
.B TCLRS_REPL_MODE=vi
or
.B mode = "vi"
under
.B [repl]
in
.I ~/.tclrs/config.toml
selects modal editing.
.SH "EDITOR SERVERS"
.B tclrs \-\-lsp
speaks the Language Server Protocol on stdio: diagnostics (the parser's failure
and then the compiler's, so the editor reports what running the file would
report), completion, hover, signature help and document symbols. The vocabulary
is the compiler's own tables, and a test fails if a name is offered that the
compiler does not know.
.PP
.B tclrs \-\-dap
speaks the Debug Adapter Protocol on stdio: breakpoints, stepping, stack frame,
variables, and the program's output as events. Stopping is compiled in \(em a
line marker before every command, emitted only for a debug compilation \(em so
an ordinary run pays nothing, and a breakpoint inside a procedure is reachable.
.SH "INLINE RUST"
A
.B rust { ... }
block compiles to a shared library and its
.B pub extern "C" fn
exports become commands. The block is rewritten out of the source before
parsing, compiled while the script is lowered (this frontend resolves dispatch
at compile time, so the name must be known before the VM starts), and cached by
the hash of its body. A procedure of the same name shadows an exported
function. Signatures are fusevm's marshalling set: up to four
.B i64
returning
.BR i64 ,
up to three
.B f64
returning
.BR f64 ,
and
.B *const c_char
returning
.B i64
or
.BR "*const c_char" .
.SH COMPILATION
.TP
.B JIT
Every VM arms fusevm's three tiers. A hot loop inside a procedure reaches a
compiled trace, because its variables are frame slots; the same loop at a
script's top level reaches nothing, because a top-level variable is a VM
global.
.B \-\-tiers
reports which tiers a given script actually reached, rather than asserting it.
.TP
.B AOT
.B \-\-aot
lowers the whole chunk through fusevm's closed-world compiler and links it into
a native executable with no parser and no bytecode dispatch loop inside it.
Refused for a script using
.B catch
or a coroutine, which need a driver outside
.BR VM::run .
.SH "WHAT IS REFUSED"
A construct that is not built yet is refused by name \(em
.RI \(dq " ... " \(dq
.B is not supported yet
\(em rather than silently misread. That includes
.BR "string wordend" / wordstart ,
the
.B array
search-token subcommands,
several
.B dict
subcommands, every
.B info
subcommand but
.BR coroutine ,
.B switch \-matchvar
and
.B return \-code
other than
.B ok
or
.BR error .
.SH TESTING
Every program in the differential suites is executed by both
.BR tclsh (1)
and tclrs and the outputs compared byte for byte; the suites skip with a note
when no tclsh is installed. A differential fuzzer generates terminating
programs and compares the two engines, and the corpus of divergences it found is
replayed as a regression suite.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR tclrs (1),
.BR tclsh (1).
.PP
Reference, engineering report and command reference:
https://menketechnologies.github.io/tclrs/ .
.br
